  "extracted_items": {
    "callouts": [
      {
        "type": "pre-read-questions",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "- What is the difference between simple note-taking and creating a \"knowledge asset\"?\n- How do I currently find old notes in my system? What fails in that process?\n- What does \"metadata\" mean to me in the context of my own notes?",
        "line_number": 26,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 476,
        "char_end": 736
      },
      {
        "type": "the-purpose",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "The purpose of this report is to deconstruct this \"Content-Context-Metadata\" triad. We will explore the theoretical underpinnings of *why* this system works and provide a practical framework for creating knowledge assets that are not only findable but remain intelligible, valuable, and generative for decades to come.",
        "line_number": 47,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 3960,
        "char_end": 4298
      },
      {
        "type": "principle-point",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "An asset is **durable** if it remains intelligible and valuable long after the short-term memory of its creation has vanished. Atomicity and clarity are the prerequisites for this durability.",
        "line_number": 73,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 6779,
        "char_end": 6994
      },
      {
        "type": "analogy",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "Think of a note as a neuron. A single, isolated neuron is physiologically useless. Its entire purpose and power come from its *synapses*—its connections (links) to other neurons. This web of connections forms a pattern of thought (the **Context**).",
        "line_number": 85,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 7818,
        "char_end": 8082
      },
      {
        "type": "definition",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "**Metadata** is the structured information *about* an asset, not *in* it. It is the label on the box, not the contents of the box. It is designed for *systems* (like search engines or queries) to read.",
        "line_number": 92,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 8273,
        "char_end": 8493
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "A practical workflow for a new idea:\n1.  **Capture (Content):** You read a fascinating article. You jot down the core idea in your own words in a new note. (`status: [🌱seed]`)\n1.  **Process (Content):** You refine the note, give it an atomic title, and flesh out the concept.\n1.  **Connect (Context):** You link it to your existing notes on `[[Related Concept A]]` and `[[Author B]]`.\n1.  **Tag (Metadata):** You complete the YAML:\n    - `aliases: \"The concept's other name\"`\n    - `tags: \"subject/main-topic\"`\n    - `source: \"[Author (Year), Title]\"`\n    - `status: \"[🌿growing]\"`",
        "line_number": 132,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 10728,
        "char_end": 11340
      },
      {
        "type": "connection-ideas",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "Sketch a mind-map linking the \"Content-Context-Metadata\" triad to these concepts:\n- `[[Zettelkasten]]`\n- `[[Cognitive Load Theory]]`\n- `[[Information Architecture]]`\n- `[[Library Science]]`",
        "line_number": 168,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 13264,
        "char_end": 13486
      },
      {
        "type": "counter-argument",
        "title": "Untitled",
        "body": "A common critique is that this level of \"note hygiene\" is obsessive, a form of productive procrastination. The counter-argument, however, is that the *upfront* cost of structuring an asset (a 2-minute tax) pays *compounding dividends* in future discoverability and utility, saving exponential time and cognitive load later.",
        "line_number": 185,
        "source": "pkb-report-core-principles-creating-durable-and-discoverable-knowledge-assets-20251120092640.md",
        "char_start": 14433,
        "char_end": 14781
      }
    ],
